	import Statement from '$lib/components/Statement.svelte';
	import Proof from '$lib/components/Proof.svelte';
	import * as antipode from './statements/antipode.md';
	import * as cayley from './statements/cayley.md';
	import * as quotient from './statements/quotient.md';
</script>

## The problem

skoro holds a rotation as a unit quaternion, and the unit quaternions cover the rotations twice:
$q$ and $-q$ are one rotation and two points of $S^3$. The type skoro declares is $S^3$ itself, with
the stereographic chart, and that chart tells the two apart, so
[the rotations' mathematics](../rotations/math.svx#the-double-cover) asks the caller to give an
observed quaternion the sign of its prediction before it is observed, by aligning it on the host.
One given with the other sign is not refused. Its residual is the coordinate of a point beside the
one point the chart at the prediction cannot reach, of length at least $4$, and growing without
bound as the estimate improves.

Nothing in a model's data can give the right sign, because the right sign is not a property of the
measurement. It is set by the prediction, and the prediction's sign by the signs of the estimated
states it is computed from, which are themselves whatever the estimate's history made them. A robot
that drives one loop and closes it shows the difficulty at its plainest: its odometry, composed from
the start, turns its heading through $2\pi$, and the composed quaternion at the end is near $-1$,
while the loop closure measures a relative rotation near the identity, whose quaternion a file or a
program, given the choice, writes with $w > 0$. At states composed from the odometry, that loop
closure is on the far side of its prediction. At states read from a file, whose signs are as
arbitrary as the measurements', the far side falls on whichever edges the signs put it; but, as
[In a cycle](#what-a-misaligned-measurement-does) shows, the cycle as a whole cannot be satisfied
with every measurement as given, wherever the states start. Both of skoro's examples draw their
measurements from the model, so their signs are right by construction, and this path has not met
outside data.

The sign carries no information about the world: a sensor or a file measured a rotation, and the
quaternion is one of its two names. The trouble is that skoro's type is the cover rather than the
group. This entry works out what a misaligned quaternion does under the present chart, and then
makes the types the groups themselves: $SO(3)$, $SE(3)$ and $SE_2(3)$, still stored as quaternions,
with the Cayley chart, whose coordinates are the same for $q$ and $-q$. A stored quaternion is then
a representative of its rotation, either sign as good as the other, and nothing in a model needs to
align one. The core's embedded types are submanifolds of their storage, which the rotations stored
as quaternions cannot be, so the core learns to declare a type stored up to a finite group. Last,
it works out how a measurement given in the format of g2o, the usual source of pose-graph datasets,
becomes one of skoro's.

## Setting

The unit quaternions $S^3$, their product $\otimes$, the covering map $\pi : S^3 \to SO(3)$ with
$\pi(q) = \pi(-q)$, the stereographic chart [](eq:archive/rotations/math/quaternion-chart) with its
inverse, and its volume are those of
[the rotations' mathematics](../rotations/math.svx#the-unit-quaternions). Writing $\bar \mu \otimes q
= (w, u)$, the stereographic coordinate of $q$ at $\mu$ is $4u / (1 + w)$, of length $4 \tan(\theta /
4)$ for $\theta \in [0, 2\pi)$ the angle of $\bar \mu \otimes q$ on $S^3$'s scale, and its chart at
$\mu$ is a diffeomorphism of $\R^3$ onto $S^3 \setminus \{-\mu\}$. The rigid motions and the extended
poses are stored as $(q, p)$ and $(q, v, p)$, with the charts of
[%full](statement:archive/rotations/math/motions), whose inverse at $(q, p)$ is

$$
	(q', p') \ominus (q, p) = \bigl(q' \ominus q,\ \pi(q)\transpose (p' - p)\bigr) ,
$$

the rotation's coordinate first, then the rest, each in the frame of the point. A tuple of vectors,
such as $(\tangent_R, \tangent_T)$, is the column vector with its entries stacked. For a point of any
of the three, $-q$ is the point whose quaternion is negated and whose other coordinates are kept;
it is the same rotation, motion or extended pose.

The normal $\mathcal N(\mu, \Sigma)$ is that of [its mathematics](../normal/math.svx#the-definition),
with the precision $\Lambda = \Sigma^{-1} = W\transpose W$, its residual $\point \ominus \mu$, and
the density with respect to the measure $\nu$ the chart normalizes,

$$
	\density_\mu(\point) = \frac{\det W}{(2\pi)^{\dimension / 2}} \exp\Bigl(-\tfrac12 \bigl\lVert W (\point \ominus \mu) \bigr\rVert^2\Bigr) e^{-\lambda(\point \ominus \mu)} , @tag(density)
$$

with $\lambda$ the chart's log-volume, $-3 \log(1 + \lVert \tangent_R \rVert^2 / 16)$ for the
stereographic chart. A measurement $z$ of a state $\point$ is predicted by a map $h(\point)$, such
as the relative motion $h(\point) = X_i^{-1} X_j$ of a pose graph, whose quaternion is $\bar q_i
\otimes q_j$.

## The antipode in the stereographic chart

Everything about the sign follows from where the chart at $\mu$ puts the two points over one
rotation.

<Statement {...antipode} />

<Proof>

Write $\bar\mu \otimes q = (w, u)$, so that $\tangent = 4u / (1 + w)$ and, since $\lVert u
\rVert^2 = 1 - w^2$,

$$
	\lVert \tangent \rVert^2 = \frac{16 \lVert u \rVert^2}{(1 + w)^2} = \frac{16 (1 - w)}{1 + w} ,
$$

which is below $16$ exactly when $w = \langle q, \mu \rangle > 0$. Both $\bar\mu \otimes (-q)$ and $\overline{(-\mu)} \otimes q$ are $(-w,
-u)$, whose coordinate is $-4u / (1 - w)$, and

$$
	-\frac{16}{\lVert \tangent \rVert^2}\, \tangent = -\frac{(1 + w)}{(1 - w)} \cdot \frac{4u}{1 + w} = -\frac{4u}{1 - w} .
$$

The lengths follow from [%full](statement:archive/rotations/math/chart): the point on the side of
$\mu$ is at the angle $\theta \in [0, \pi]$ from $\mu$ on $S^3$'s scale of angles, which is the
angle between the rotations, and the other at $2\pi - \theta$, whose coordinate has the length
$4 \tan(\pi/2 - \theta/4) = 4 \cot(\theta/4)$.

</Proof>

So the map from one sign to the other is the inversion in the sphere of radius $4$ followed by
negation. The ball of radius $4$ is the chart of the near sides, one point for each rotation within
less than a half-turn of $\pi(\mu)$, and its complement the far sides; the sphere of radius $4$
itself holds the points orthogonal to $\mu$, the half-turns, where the two signs have coordinates
$\tangent$ and $-\tangent$ of equal length. This sharpens the third claim of
[%full](statement:archive/rotations/math/cover), that the two lengths multiply to $16$.

## What a misaligned measurement does

Let $z$ be a measured rotation given with the sign on the far side of its prediction, so that the
point on the near side is $-z$, at the angle $\varphi \in [0, \pi]$ from $\pi(h(\point))$. Its
residual has the length $4 \cot(\varphi/4)$, which grows without bound as $\varphi \to 0$: the term
is least likely exactly where the estimate is right. For a concentrated normal, with $\sigma^2 <
\tfrac83$, its density [](eq:density) increases with $\varphi$, and its maximum is at $h(\point) = z$, the measured rotation on the other
sheet, which the estimate reaches from where it is only by turning through the remaining $2\pi -
\varphi$.

**Against Gauss–Newton.** Along the geodesic from $h(\point)$ to $z$, let $\theta = 2\pi - \varphi$ be
the angle that remains, so that the residual is $r(\theta) = 4 \tan(\theta / 4)$. A Gauss–Newton
step on this residual alone changes $\theta$ by

$$
	-\frac{r(\theta)}{r'(\theta)} = -4 \tan\frac\theta4 \cos^2 \frac\theta4 = -2 \sin\frac\theta2 = -2 \sin\frac\varphi2 ,
$$

so that $\varphi$ becomes $\varphi + 2 \sin(\varphi/2)$, about $2\varphi$: each step doubles the
error. Its weight in the normal equations, $r'(\theta)^2 / \sigma^2 = \sigma^{-2} \sin^{-4}(\varphi
/ 4)$, is about $256 / (\sigma^2 \varphi^4)$, which overwhelms the weight of every correctly given
measurement once the estimate is close. A misaligned measurement is not an outlier that the others
outvote; near the right answer it is the term that decides the step, and it steps away.

**In a cycle.** In a pose graph the misalignment of one measurement is the misalignment of a cycle.
Take the loop of [The problem](#the-problem) with every rotation about one axis, so that the
quaternions of the states are $(\cos(\phi_k / 2), \sin(\phi_k / 2)\, e_3)$ and angles add on $S^3$
as they do on the line, modulo $4\pi$. Let the $n$ odometry measurements turn by $\alpha_1, \dots,
\alpha_n$, with $\sum_k \alpha_k = 2\pi$, and the loop closure by $\beta = 0$, each given with $w >
0$. The residual angles $\theta_e$ of the $n + 1$ edges, each in $(-2\pi, 2\pi)$, telescope around
the cycle:

$$
	\sum_e \theta_e \equiv \sum_k \alpha_k + \beta = 2\pi \pmod{4\pi} .
$$

No estimate makes every residual zero. With equal noise $\sigma$ on every edge, the cost of an edge
is $8 \sigma^{-2} \tan^2(\theta / 4) + 6 \log \cos(\theta / 4)$, the negative logarithm of
[](eq:density) up to a constant, whose second derivative is at least $\sigma^{-2} \sec^4(\theta/4) -
\tfrac38 \sec^2(\theta/4)$, positive when $\sigma^2 < \tfrac83$. The cost is then strictly convex,
and the least total cost under the constraint spreads the turn evenly: every edge is wrong by
$2\pi / (n + 1)$. On a loop of a hundred edges that is $3.6°$ an edge, a trajectory that looks
plausible and has an extra full turn of heading wound into it. Had the loop closure been given as
$-z$, with $\beta = 2\pi$, the sum would be $0$ modulo $4\pi$ and every residual could vanish.

So a measured rotation's sign is a choice, for each cycle it closes, of whether the estimate winds
once more around it. Choosing the sign on the near side of a prediction chooses the winding of the
estimate the prediction was computed at, which unwinds the drift the shorter way.

## A chart that cannot tell the signs apart

The stereographic chart is a chart of $S^3$, and every chart of $S^3$ must tell $q$ from $-q$, since
they are different points of it. A chart of $SO(3)$ must not, and the Cayley chart, already in
[the note's table](../../math/chart-volume/#the-usual-charts) as a global and full chart of the
rotations, is written on quaternions as the familiar first-order update, normalized.

<Statement {...cayley} />

<Proof>

The norm of $(1, \tangent / 2)$ is $\sqrt{1 + \lVert \tangent \rVert^2 / 4}$, so $\gamma(\tangent)
\in S^3$, with $w(\gamma) > 0$ and $\lVert \operatorname{vec}(\gamma) \rVert / w(\gamma) =
\lVert \tangent \rVert / 2$; it is the rotation by $\theta$ about $\tangent$ with $\tan(\theta / 2) =
\lVert \tangent \rVert / 2$, so $\theta \in [0, \pi)$, every such rotation once. Negating $q$
negates $q \otimes \gamma$. Negating $p$ or $q$ negates $\bar q \otimes p$, whose $\operatorname{vec}$
and $w$ change sign together, so their ratio does not. For $p = q \otimes \gamma(\tangent)$, $\bar q
\otimes p = \gamma(\tangent)$ and $2 \operatorname{vec}(\gamma) / w(\gamma) = \tangent$; and every
rotation by less than $\pi$ from $\pi(q)$ is $\pi(q \otimes \delta)$ for a $\delta$ with
$w(\delta) > 0$, which is $\gamma$ of $2 \operatorname{vec}(\delta) / w(\delta)$. The first-order form and the
last identity follow as for the stereographic chart
([%full](statement:archive/rotations/math/chart) and
[](eq:archive/rotations/math/conjugation)): $\gamma(\tangent) = (1, \tangent / 2) + O(\lVert \tangent
\rVert^2)$, and $\gamma$ depends on $\tangent$ only through its direction and its length.

</Proof>

The chart's coordinate of a rotation by $\theta$ has the length $2 \tan(\theta / 2) = \theta +
\theta^3 / 12 + O(\theta^5)$. Its volume against the Haar measure of $SO(3)$ normalized at the
chart's centre is

$$
	\lambda(\tangent) = -2 \log\bigl(1 + \tfrac14 \lVert \tangent \rVert^2\bigr) , @tag(cayley-volume)
$$

by [the note](../../math/chart-volume/#the-usual-charts), whose derivation in the exponential chart
is the same for a quaternion as for a matrix. Every coordinate the chart computes at $\mu$ is that of
the rotation, whichever sign either quaternion is given in.

**What it does to a measurement.** Read under the Cayley chart, a measured rotation has one
residual, of length $2 \tan(\varphi / 2)$ for $\varphi \in [0, \pi)$ the angle between the measured
and the predicted rotations, and none of [What a misaligned measurement does](#what-a-misaligned-measurement-does)
can happen. The residual is unbounded as the prediction nears a half-turn from the measurement, a
real error of a half-turn rather than a mistaken sign, and there it attracts rather than repels: a
Gauss–Newton step on $r(\varphi) = 2 \tan(\varphi / 2)$ alone changes $\varphi$ by
$-r / r' = -\sin \varphi$, toward $0$, if slowly near $\pi$. In the cycle of the one-turn loop, the
residual angles are rotation angles, in $(-\pi, \pi)$, and add modulo $2\pi$,

$$
	\sum_e \theta_e \equiv \sum_k \alpha_k + \beta = 2\pi \equiv 0 \pmod{2\pi} ,
$$

so every residual can vanish: the winding that the stereographic chart forced on the estimate is
no longer a property of any point of the type.

## A type stored up to a group

The Cayley chart is not a chart of $S^3$, and the core cannot declare it as one. Its retraction and
inverse meet every condition of [%full](statement:archive/core/math/embedded) on $\manifold = S^3$:
$R(q, 0) = q$, $R$ stays on $S^3$, its derivative $B(q) = q \otimes (0, \tfrac12 I)$ has rank $3$,
and $Q(q, R(q, \tangent)) = \tangent$. The derivative maps that statement gives are therefore right
as they stand. What fails is what [the core's measures](../measures/math.svx#what-an-embedded-type-declares)
ask of a type with a volume. On $S^3$, the chart at $\mu$ reaches only the open hemisphere $\langle
q, \mu \rangle > 0$, so it is not full, and $Q(\mu, \cdot)$, finite on the other hemisphere, is not
the inverse of the chart there; it is exactly the chart that measures' decision
[Only full charts declare a volume](../measures/math.svx#what-an-embedded-type-declares) refuses,
because a density read at a point it does not reach would be silently wrong. Read on the rotations
instead, the chart is full and $Q$ is right everywhere, since the point $Q$ reads on the far
hemisphere is the same rotation as its antipode on the near one. The type is $SO(3)$, and $S^3$ is
how it is stored.

$SO(3)$ is not a submanifold of $\R^4$, or of any coordinate space in which the quaternions store
it: the quotient of $S^3$ by $\pm 1$ is the real projective space $\mathbb{RP}^3$, which does not
embed in $\R^4$. So the core's definition of an embedded type, a submanifold of its storage, has to
admit a type whose storage covers it.

<Statement {...quotient} />

<Proof>

A finite group acting freely by diffeomorphisms acts properly discontinuously, so $N$ is a manifold
and $\varpi$ a covering, a local diffeomorphism. The retraction on $N$ is well defined, since
$\varpi(R(g \point, \tangent)) = \varpi(g\, R(\point, \tangent)) = \varpi(R(\point, \tangent))$; it is
smooth, the identity at $\tangent = 0$, and of full rank there, since $\varpi$ is a local
diffeomorphism and $R$ meets the same conditions on $\manifold$. The inverse is well defined by the
two invariances of $Q$, and inverts the retraction near $0$ as $Q$ inverts $R$. For the derivatives,
$f(R(g \point, \tangent)) = f(g\, R(\point, \tangent)) = f(R(\point, \tangent))$ as functions of
$\tangent$, and $f \circ R(\point, \cdot) = \bar f \circ \varpi \circ R(\point, \cdot)$, which is
$\bar f$ in the chart of $N$ at $[\point]$.

</Proof>

A chart volume is declared for such a type as [%full](statement:archive/measures/math/declaration)
declares one, with $\manifold$ read as $N$: the measure is the one the retraction normalizes on
$N$; the domain, the image, globality and fullness are those of $\tangent \mapsto [\mu] \oplus
\tangent$ in $N$; and the log-volume $\Lambda$ is asked to be invariant, $\Lambda(g \mu, \tangent) =
\Lambda(\mu, \tangent)$. Since $\varpi$ is a local diffeomorphism and the chart volume is local, the
volume on $N$ is that of the chart on $\manifold$ wherever the chart is injective there, and a
density on $N$ read at a stored point is the same at every representative, since it reads the point
only through $Q$ and $\Lambda$.

**What a model may read.** Everything the core computes from a quantity of such a type goes through
$R$, $Q$, $P$ and $\Lambda$, and is the same at every representative. Two things a model writes need
not be. The inclusion $\iota$ reads a stored point as its storage, which is a representative and
not a function of the point of $N$: on the rotations, the quaternion itself. A map out of the type
written on the inclusion is a map of $N$ only if it is invariant under $G$, as $\pi(q)$, a rotated
vector and the Cayley coordinate are, and the rotation vector of [](eq:archive/rotations/math/log),
of length up to $2\pi$, is not. A map into the type, written on the inclusions and projected as
[](eq:archive/core/math/map-into) does, is a map of $N$ when it is equivariant, $F_{\mathrm s}(g_1
\point_1, \dots, g_m \point_m) \in G\, F_{\mathrm s}(\point_1, \dots, \point_m)$; the composition
$P(q_1 \otimes q_2)$ is, since $(\pm q_1) \otimes (\pm q_2) = \pm (q_1 \otimes q_2)$. The core cannot
check invariance, which is a property of a composite on all its inputs, any more than it checks that
a type's $R$ stays on $\manifold$; a type's entry says which of its maps are maps of $N$, and its
tests check them at both representatives.

**Which representative a value carries** is decided by how it was made, and matters to nothing that
reads it as a point of $N$. A retraction $\mu \oplus \tangent$ is on the side of the stored $\mu$,
$\langle \mu \oplus \tangent, \mu \rangle > 0$; a draw of the normal therefore carries its mean's
sign; a composition carries the product's sign; and an observed or given value carries the sign it
was given in.

**Products.** A product of types stored up to groups $G_1, \dots, G_m$ is stored up to their
product, acting factor by factor, and meets the conditions of [%full](statement:quotient) when each
factor does. Its chart, its volume and its normal are the product's, with nothing to sum over: the
charts are invariant already.

## The rotations, the rigid motions and the extended poses

The three types become the groups.

- **The rotations**, $SO(3)$, stored as $S^3 \subset \R^4$ up to $G = \{\pm 1\}$, with the Cayley
  retraction and inverse of [%full](statement:cayley), the normalizing projection $P(x) = x / \lVert
  x \rVert$, and the log-volume [](eq:cayley-volume). The retraction is equivariant and the inverse
  invariant by [%full](statement:cayley), $P(-x) = -P(x)$, and the log-volume reads $\tangent$
  alone.
- **The rigid motions**, $SE(3)$, stored as $S^3 \times \R^3$ up to $(q, p) \mapsto (-q, p)$, with
  $(q, p) \oplus (\phi, \rho) = (q \oplus \phi,\ p + \pi(q)\, \rho)$ and its inverse $(q' \ominus q,\
  \pi(q)\transpose (p' - p))$, both meeting the conditions because $\pi(-q) = \pi(q)$, and the
  log-volume of the rotation alone, against $\nu_{SO(3)} \times$ Lebesgue, a Haar measure by the
  argument of [%full](statement:archive/rotations/math/motions).
- **The extended poses**, $SE_2(3)$, stored as $S^3 \times \R^3 \times \R^3$ up to the same sign,
  likewise.

Each chart is global and full on its group: the rotation's reaches every rotation by less than $\pi$
from the point's, which is all of $SO(3)$ but the half-turns, a set of measure zero, and the
translations' are affine bijections. So the normal applies to them as
[its mathematics](../normal/math.svx#setting) defines it, its density and its sampler exact, and its
law is the law of the rotation. A normal's term is the same whichever sign the value and the mean are
given in, so an observed quaternion needs no alignment, and the sign of a state's quaternion is a
choice of representative that no term reads.

**What the change of chart costs.** The figures of the rotations'
[normal on these types](../rotations/math.svx#the-normal-on-these-types) follow from the log-volume,
whose negative is now $2 \log(1 + \lVert \tangent \rVert^2 / 4) = \tfrac12 \lVert \tangent \rVert^2 +
O(\lVert \tangent \rVert^4)$, with the Hessian $I$ in place of $\tfrac38 I$:

- a measurement of a rotation weighs on the state as a Gaussian of its residual would with the
  precision $\Lambda - I$ on the rotation's block, a change of $1\,\%$ for a noise of $0.1$ rad;
- the mean is a mode of the density when $\sigma^2 < 1$, a standard deviation under $1$ rad, where
  it was $1.6$ rad;
- Gauss–Newton with the log-volume's gradient converges at a rate within $\kappa = \sigma^2$ of the
  residuals' own.

A broad normal is no better than before. Its draws go to the half-turns as its scale grows, since
$\theta = 2 \arctan(\lVert \varepsilon \rVert / 2) \to \pi$, where the stereographic chart's went to the
far side of $S^3$, which as a rotation is the mean again; neither is a way to say that an attitude
is unknown, which a uniform law will be. The coordinate is farther from the angle at third order,
$\theta^3 / 12$ against $\theta^3 / 48$, which a model meets only through the log-volume's
curvature above. The [double cover](../rotations/math.svx#the-double-cover)'s law on the rotations,
$\density(q) + \density(-q)$, and its mirrored modes, are gone: a normal on $SO(3)$ is a law on
$SO(3)$ already.

**The maps of the rotations.** Every map of [the rotations' mathematics](../rotations/math.svx#maps-of-the-rotations)
that reads the rotation through $\pi$ or through a composition stays as it is: the group operations
and the actions, the matrices and the adjoints, the exponential into the type, the Jacobians, the
rotation vector of the shorter turn, the angle between two rotations, the shorter interpolation,
the Euler angles where their formulas are even in $q$, and the constructions into the type, whose
sign was a choice anyway. Those that read the representative are maps of $S^3$ and not of $SO(3)$:
the logarithm with its length in $[0, 2\pi)$ and the axis and angle read off it, the interpolation by
the arc that avoids $-q$, and alignment, which chooses a representative. Each has a counterpart on
$SO(3)$ among the first: the rotation vector of the shorter turn, its axis and an angle in $[0, \pi]$,
and the shorter interpolation.

> [!DECISION] The rotations are the group, stored as quaternions up to sign
> The rotations, the rigid motions and the extended poses are $SO(3)$, $SE(3)$ and $SE_2(3)$, stored
> as unit quaternions with their translations, up to the sign of the quaternion, under the Cayley
> chart. Their charts are global and full on the groups, their normal is the law of the rotation, and
> no observed quaternion is aligned. What the stereographic chart bought, a slightly closer
> coordinate and a slightly flatter volume, is less than what it cost: a type that was the cover,
> every observed quaternion aligned by its caller, and a model that could wind a turn into an
> estimate without notice. The core gains the declaration of a type stored up to a finite group,
> which changes none of its derivatives and only reads its measures' conditions on the quotient.

## Measurements as g2o gives them

The pose-graph datasets of the literature are files in the format of the g2o library, whose 3D lines
are

```text
VERTEX_SE3:QUAT i  x y z  qx qy qz qw
EDGE_SE3:QUAT   i j  x y z  qx qy qz qw  Ω₁₁ Ω₁₂ … Ω₁₆ Ω₂₂ … Ω₆₆
```

a vertex's initial pose and an edge's measurement $Z$ of $X_i^{-1} X_j$, each a translation and a
quaternion with $w$ last, followed by the upper triangle of the edge's $6 \times 6$ information
matrix $\Omega$, row by row. As g2o's source reads and scores these lines (`EdgeSE3::read` and
`EdgeSE3::computeError`, read on its `master` branch on 2026-11-11), the quaternion is normalized on
reading and its sign kept, and the error is

$$
	e = \bigl(\, t(\Delta),\ \operatorname{vec}\bigl(s_\Delta\, q(\Delta)\bigr) \bigr),
	\qquad \Delta = Z^{-1} X_i^{-1} X_j , @tag(g2o-error)
$$

the translation of $\Delta$ followed by the vector part of its quaternion, read off its rotation
matrix and given the sign $s_\Delta$ that makes $w \ge 0$. The information matrix is that of $e$, in
this order. The vertex's increment is applied on the right, in the frame of the pose, as skoro's
chart applies it.

**The same cut, the same blindness to sign.** g2o's error reads the measurement through the matrix
of $\Delta$, so the sign in which $Z$ is written never reaches it, and its rotation coordinate is a
function of the Cayley coordinate. skoro observes $Z$ with the mean $h = X_i^{-1} X_j$ and reads the
residual $\tangent = Z \ominus h$, rotation first. Since $\bar h \otimes Z$ is the conjugate of $\bar Z
\otimes h$, the two have the same $w$ and opposite vector parts, and with $|w| = (1 - \lVert e_R
\rVert^2)^{1/2}$ the sign $s_\Delta$ makes positive, the rotation coordinates are related exactly,
and the translations to first order, $p_h - p_Z$ being $-\pi(q_h)\, \tangent_T$:

$$
	\begin{gathered}
		\tangent_R = \frac{2 \operatorname{vec}(\bar h \otimes Z)}{w(\bar h \otimes Z)} = -\frac{2\, e_R}{\sqrt{1 - \lVert e_R \rVert^2}} , \\
		e_T = \pi(q_Z)\transpose (p_h - p_Z) = -\pi(q_Z)\transpose \pi(q_h)\, \tangent_T = -\tangent_T + O(\lVert \tangent \rVert^2) .
	\end{gathered} @tag(g2o-coordinates)
$$

Both coordinates are cut at the half-turns, where $w = 0$, $\lVert e_R \rVert = 1$ and $\tangent_R$
is unbounded. g2o's law is not a normal: it is the likelihood factor $\exp(-\tfrac12 e\transpose
\Omega e)$ of [the normal's mathematics](../normal/math.svx#without-a-volume-or-a-global-chart),
with no volume, so it has no sampler of the normal's form and its normalization depends on
$\Omega$.

**Its information in skoro's coordinates.** By [](eq:g2o-coordinates), $e = -A \tangent +
O(\lVert \tangent \rVert^2)$ with $A(\tangent_R, \tangent_T) = (\tangent_T, \tfrac12 \tangent_R)$,
and the precision of $\tangent$ that gives the same quadratic form to second order is

$$
	\Lambda = A\transpose \Omega A =
	\begin{bmatrix}
		\tfrac14 \Omega_{RR} & \tfrac12 \Omega_{RT} \\
		\tfrac12 \Omega_{TR} & \Omega_{TT}
	\end{bmatrix} , @tag(g2o-precision)
$$

with $\Omega_{TT}$ the first three rows and columns of $\Omega$, $\Omega_{RR}$ the last three, and
$\Omega_{TR} = \Omega_{RT}\transpose$ the block between them. The rotation's precision is a quarter
of g2o's, because g2o's coordinate is half an angle.

**What the conversion costs.** The two costs agree at a zero residual to second order and differ in
the third: by [](eq:g2o-coordinates), g2o's rotation coordinate has the length $\sin(\theta / 2) =
\theta/2 - \theta^3/48 + \cdots$, and half of skoro's the length $\tan(\theta / 2) = \theta/2 +
\theta^3/24 + \cdots$. The normal adds its log-volume, which changes the rotation's precision by
$I$, negligible when $\Lambda_{RR}$ is large against it. So an estimate under
[](eq:g2o-precision) agrees with g2o's to the order of the residuals at the solution, not exactly,
and a comparison with g2o's solution on a dataset is a comparison to that tolerance.

## In skoro's terms

- **A type stored up to a group** is declared by its storage, its dimension, $R$, $Q$, optionally
  $P$, and the finite group $G$ of linear maps of the storage it is stored up to, meeting
  [%full](statement:quotient); a type that declares no group is stored up to the trivial one, as
  every type is today. Its quantities are points of $N$; their stored values are representatives.
- **Its derivatives** are those [%full](statement:archive/core/math/embedded) gives, unchanged,
  computed at the stored representative; by [%full](statement:quotient) they are the same at every
  representative for every function the core computes.
- **Its volume** is declared as for any embedded type, with the domain, globality and fullness read
  on $N$ and the log-volume invariant under $G$; its normal is the normal of
  [its mathematics](../normal/math.svx#the-definition), with no change.
- **The rotations, the rigid motions and the extended poses** are the groups, stored up to the sign
  of their quaternion, with the Cayley charts, the normalizing projection, and the log-volume
  [](eq:cayley-volume), all composites of the core's primitives. A quantity of these types is a
  rotation, motion or extended pose; a measured one is observed in whatever sign it was given, and
  scored by the normal of its prediction; a state's prior is a normal like any other.
- **Maps out of these types** are maps of the groups when they are invariant under the sign, and
  maps into them when they are equivariant; the rotations' maps are sorted as
  [The maps of the rotations](#the-rotations-the-rigid-motions-and-the-extended-poses) says.
- **A measurement read from g2o** is a quaternion and a translation in g2o's order, normalized, in
  whatever sign the file gives, observed under the normal with the precision [](eq:g2o-precision);
  a vertex's pose is a state's initial value, in whatever sign the file gives.
- **Alignment on the host** is no longer needed by a model; a quaternion is given $w \ge 0$, when a
  user wants one to report, by choosing the representative.

## Open questions

> [!TODO] The maps of the representative
> The logarithm with its length in $[0, 2\pi)$, the axis and angle read off it, the interpolation
> along the arc that avoids $-q$, alignment, and the inclusion itself read the stored quaternion
> rather than the rotation. Either the rotations keep them, documented as maps of the
> representative, whose value depends on the sign a point happens to carry; or they are removed,
> their $SO(3)$ counterparts taking their names, and the inclusion of these types is read only
> through invariant maps such as $\pi$. Removing them is the safer rule, since a term built on one
> would make a density depend on the sign again.
